      "text": "For Exothermic and endothermic reactions, a board-ready response should be built from observation to chemical explanation. Begin by identifying the reactants and products, then state what has changed at the particle level. If an equation is relevant, write the correct formulae before balancing. Count each element on both sides and alter coefficients only. If the question asks for a condition, place heat, light, electricity or a catalyst above the arrow as appropriate. If an observation is asked, distinguish gas evolution, precipitate formation, colour change and temperature change rather than using the single phrase “a chemical change occurs”. For classification, look at the number and nature of reactants and products: a combination reaction has one product, a decomposition reaction has one reactant, displacement depends on relative reactivity, and double displacement involves exchange of ions. Redox questions require identifying which substance is oxidised and which is reduced; oxidation and reduction are paired processes. Corrosion and rancidity should be remembered as useful real-world examples of unwanted oxidation, along with methods used to slow them. This expanded note is intended for board preparation: learn the principle, write the representative equation, identify the observation or product, and practise applying the same idea to a changed situation. Accuracy matters more than decorative wording. Chemical formulae, subscripts, coefficients, conditions and terminology should be checked before the final answer is written."
